Team Care Arrangement (Allied Health Referral)
General Practice Other
Complex chronic condition, Requirement for multidisciplinary intervention, Generation of structured management plan, Collaborative goal setting

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-044
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe functional decline and psychological crisis following a recent hospitalization, requiring immediate multidisciplinary coordination to prevent rapid readmission.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, live-in adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient recently discharged following a severe exacerbation of severe COPD and osteoarthritis, now experiencing a sudden, catastrophic loss of mobility and overwhelming anxiety regarding their ability to remain at home. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to stabilize the acute psychological crisis, formally assess the functional deficits, and immediately generate a structured Team Care Arrangement to secure urgent, subsidized in-home physiotherapy and occupational therapy. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in an unwarranted emergency department readmission tonight driven entirely by functional failure and panic.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I've been home for two days, I can't even walk to the toilet anymore, and I'm terrified I'm going to end up in a nursing home forever."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a complex chronic condition (Severe COPD and bilateral knee osteoarthritis) requiring immediate multidisciplinary intervention. Following a recent hospital discharge, there is a clear identification of specific functional deficits, notably an absolute inability to safely navigate the home environment or manage personal hygiene. The patient is in a state of acute despair. The consultation requires a collaborative discussion regarding the distinct roles of specific allied health professionals (Physiotherapy for mobility, OT for home modifications). The goal is the drafting of a structured formal chronic disease management plan and obtaining explicit patient consent for clinical information sharing among providers. There is a complete absence of an acute medical crisis (e.g., no acute respiratory failure today), but a profound functional crisis.
Functional Status: Severely restricted; currently bedbound due to fear of falling and severe deconditioning.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of falls and subsequent hospital readmission; immense caregiver burden on the adult child.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Frail, anxious, remaining strictly in bed, visibly overwhelmed by the situation.
Physical Exam: Respiratory: Reduced breath sounds globally consistent with severe COPD, no acute wheeze or crackles. Musculoskeletal: Severe bilateral crepitus and restricted range of motion in the knees. The assessment focuses on the goal of improving long-term health outcomes and self-management capabilities, requiring the generation of the required administrative documentation for subsidized allied health visits. The patient is physiologically stable, confirming a lack of requirement for acute hospital admission. Expected investigation results include documented functional improvement or attainment of specific therapeutic goals assessed by the allied health professional upon completion of the structured referral cycle.
Environmental Observations: Home environment is cluttered, multiple trip hazards, bathroom lacks safety rails.
Medication Reconciliation: Tiotropium and regular Paracetamol verified; compliance is adequate.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Team Care Arrangement Creation (Secondary to Acute Functional Crisis) - Status: Stable (Physiologically)
Differentials:

Acute Exacerbation of COPD - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Stable oxygen saturations (94% on room air is baseline for this patient), normal respiratory rate, and absence of increased purulent sputum or severe acute dyspnea at rest.

Acute Delirium / Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, alert and oriented cognition, and the distress is entirely situational and appropriate to the sudden loss of functional independence.

Acute Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral calf swelling, erythema, or deep palpable tenderness in the lower extremities.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Severe Falls Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe, acute functional decompensation and psychological crisis immediately following a hospital discharge, driven by physical deconditioning and environmental hazards. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of imminent, unnecessary hospital readmission due to coping failure. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to perform a thorough physical examination (ruling out acute medical illness), provide immediate psychological stabilization, and formally draft a Team Care Arrangement. Generating this legal framework immediately unlocked access to urgent, subsidized allied health interventions, fundamentally securing the patient's ability to remain safely in their own home and neutralizing the crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a comprehensive functional assessment. Drafted a formal Team Care Arrangement (MBS Item 723) incorporating a Physiotherapist and an Occupational Therapist. Generated the referrals for urgent in-home assessments. Provided extensive verbal reassurance and collaborative goal setting focusing on independent bathroom access.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce your severe fear of falling while we wait for the physiotherapist, commit to the "anchor" method. Before you attempt to stand up from the bed or chair, you must physically place both hands firmly on a solid, immovable object (like the heavy bedside table or your walker) and count to three. By forcing a deliberate, physically anchored pause before moving, you entirely eliminate the rushed, unbalanced movements that cause the majority of falls, immediately increasing your safety and confidence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referrals faxed to community Physiotherapy and Occupational Therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Four-wheeled walker and over-toilet frame strongly recommended (OT to formalize).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 14 days to monitor the progress of the allied health interventions.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you experience a fall and are completely unable to get up, if you develop sudden, severe chest pain, or if your breathing becomes so difficult that you cannot speak in full sentences.

